<section class="ai-reveal">
  <h3 class="ai-reveal__title">Try it: Think It Through</h3>
  <p class="ai-reveal__prompt">Work through each question, then reveal a sample answer.</p>

  <details class="lesson-reveal">
    <summary>Which AI tool can be used to identify weeds in a field image?</summary>
    <p>A <strong>vision model</strong> — it functions as the "eyes," locating and identifying objects like weeds within an image before any counting or classifying happens.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="lesson-reveal">
    <summary>Which AI tool can be used to count weeds once they're identified?</summary>
    <p>A <strong>quantification model</strong> — once weeds are located, a quantification model provides the specific numeric output, such as "47 weeds detected."</p>
  </details>

  <details class="lesson-reveal">
    <summary>A drone collects 1,200 images at 20 million pixels each, and NDVI must be calculated for every pixel. How can AI help?</summary>
    <p>That's 24 billion individual NDVI calculations — far beyond what's practical by hand. AI (and the software built on it) can run that same formula automatically across every pixel in every image in a fraction of the time, then compile the results into a usable field health map.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="lesson-reveal">
    <summary>What happens if some of the data used to train an AI model is labeled wrong?</summary>
    <p>The model learns the wrong pattern. Since training depends on labeled data being accurate, mislabeled examples teach the model incorrect associations — leading to unreliable predictions, misidentifications, or reduced accuracy once it's deployed in real-world conditions. This is exactly why human expertise during labeling matters so much.</p>
  </details>
</section>

